### Swedish Employment Rules & Unemployment Policies

#### 1. Employment Protection Act (LAS)

* **Objective Grounds (*Saklig grund*):** An employer cannot unilaterally dismiss a permanent employee without valid, objective reasons (either Redundancy/*Arbetsbrist* or Personal Reasons/*Personliga skäl*). Settling via mutual agreement avoids the employer having to prove objective grounds.


* **Notice Period Standard:** Statutory notice periods range from 1 to 6 months based on tenure. For ~3 years of service, the statutory notice period is **2 months**.

#### 2. Work Permit & Migration Rules (Time-Critical)

* **3-Month Grace Period:** Once your formal end date arrives in November 2026, you have a **3-month grace period** to secure a new qualifying job offer and file a new work permit application with the Migration Agency (*Migrationsverket*).


* **Extension Reforms:** Legislative proposals extending the grace period to 6 months are scheduled to take effect around **February 1, 2027**. Unless your case falls after that date, assume the strict **3-month limit** applies to your situation.
* **Employer Notification:** Both you and your employer are required to inform Migrationsverket when the employment formally terminates.



#### 3. Unemployment Insurance (A-kassa) & Transition Support

* **Work Permit Prerequisite:** You can only receive A-kassa payouts if you hold a valid, active work permit that allows you to be registered with the Public Employment Service (*Arbetsförmedlingen*) and immediately available for work.
* **Mutual Agreement Impact:** Voluntary separations or mutual agreements can trigger a **45-working-day sanction period** from A-kassa unless the agreement explicitly states that the termination was initiated by the employer due to operational reasons (*Arbetsbrist*).
* **Severance Offset:** A-kassa views severance pay as extended salary. A 2-month severance package delays your eligibility to claim A-kassa benefits by 2 months following your end date.

